= Code in Mosaic

This example collects the supported ways to put code-like text in a
Mosaic document. Inline code uses backticks, as in `cargo mos check`
or `#set text(font: "Noto Sans")`.

Inline code can also span a soft source line break when a short phrase
needs to stay visually code-shaped in the editor and output:
`a sad student wrote
this`.

== Preformatted command transcripts

Use `#pre[...]` for terminal output, command transcripts, or other raw
text where the content is not tied to a programming language.

#pre[$ cargo mos check examples/code/main.mos
$ cargo mos build examples/code/main.mos
Finished `dev` profile]

== Code blocks

Use `#code[...]` for code examples. Mosaic preserves the raw block text
and renders it with the monospace face.

#code[fn main() {
	println!("hello, mosaic");
}]

Code blocks are raw bracket bodies. If the body needs a literal closing
bracket, escape it so the block scanner does not treat it as the end of
the block.

#code[let brackets = "open [ and escaped close \]"
...or unescaped non-close \\\];
println!(brackets);]

== Choosing a form

Use inline code for identifiers and small fragments such as `NodeKind::Raw`.
Use `#pre[...]` for pasted terminal sessions. Use `#code[...]` for source
snippets that should stand on their own.
